 ## Overview
-Shadow Ray Casting is a family of local illumination techniques that evaluate visibility between a point in a volume (or on its boundary) and a light source by tracing rays through the participating medium or geometry. It solves the problem of determining which parts of a volume are illuminated and which are in shadow, providing depth cues and realistic lighting attenuation. It differs from [Two-Pass Shadow Casting](2196-two-pass-shadow-casting-kajiya-and-von-herzen.md) by evaluating visibility per-sample during ray marching rather than via a separate pre-pass, and from [Shadow Mapping](2934-shadow-mapping.md) by avoiding depth-buffer quantization artifacts in favor of direct volumetric integration or stochastic sampling.
+Shadow Ray Casting evaluates visibility at a point $\mathbf{p}$ by casting rays toward light sources to determine occlusion by participating media or geometry [S1, unofficial][S2]. It differs from [Two-Pass Shadow Casting (Kajiya and Von Herzen)](2196-two-pass-shadow-casting-kajiya-and-von-herzen.md) by integrating transmittance directly during traversal rather than using a separate accumulation pass, and from [Shadow Mapping](2934-shadow-mapping.md) by avoiding depth-buffer quantization artifacts through explicit ray-volume intersection [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
