 ## Background
-Shadow ray casting in volume rendering originated from the need to enhance the visual perception of 3D scalar data. Early implementations focused on hard shadows cast by polygonal geometry onto volumes, often using shadow maps or pre-pass techniques. As hardware capabilities increased, stochastic methods like ambient occlusion via shadow rays and soft shadows via area lights became prevalent, allowing for more realistic lighting in participating media. The integration of these techniques into real-time pipelines, such as those using GLSL compute shaders, has further optimized performance for large-scale aerosols and structured datasets [S3].
+The concept of shadow rays originates from early ray tracing literature, where visibility was determined by casting rays from intersection points to light sources [S2]. In volume rendering, this was adapted to account for participating media, requiring integration of extinction coefficients along the ray path rather than simple geometric intersection [S1, unofficial][S2]. The method is widely used in scientific visualization and real-time rendering engines to enhance depth perception in volumetric data [S2].
 
